{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t09-013",
  "track": "track-09",
  "title": "performance profiling capacity and .NET diagnostics",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Performance work begins with a user-visible symptom and a reproducible workload. A profile samples or records resource use; it is evidence to test a bottleneck hypothesis, not a performance conclusion by itself.",
    "Latency distributions and throughput must be measured under representative concurrency, data shape, cache state, and dependency behavior. A mean can hide high-percentile regressions and coordinated omission.",
    "CPU, allocation rate, garbage collection, locks, thread-pool scheduling, I/O, network, database, and queueing interact. Optimize the measured constrained resource and remeasure the same scenario.",
    ".NET diagnostic tools communicate with runtime diagnostic ports. Permissions, process identity, container namespaces, runtime version, and dump/trace size can affect collection.",
    "Capacity planning estimates sustainable demand with safety margin and tests scaling limits. Load, stress, spike, soak, and capacity tests answer different questions; a single benchmark does not establish production capacity."
  ],
