  "accuracyFacts": [
    {
      "fact": "For .NET 9 and later, dotnet-counters can consume System.Runtime runtime metrics through System.Diagnostics.Metrics. .NET 8 and earlier do not provide that System.Runtime meter, so dotnet-counters falls back to older System.Runtime EventCounters. Do not describe both collection paths as the same API.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/dotnet-counters",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/built-in-metrics-runtime",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/available-counters"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "EventPipe is a cross-platform .NET runtime tracing mechanism for runtime and EventSource events. The ordinary dotnet-trace collect works across supported OSes without native profiler privileges; dotnet-trace collect-linux is a separate Linux-only mode requiring kernel 6.4 or later and root, with native and kernel event capabilities.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/eventpipe",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/dotnet-trace"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "The diagnostic port is a local interprocess communication endpoint: named pipes on Windows and Unix domain sockets on Linux/macOS for CoreCLR. Access is governed by OS/process/container boundaries and diagnostic-port configuration; container namespace and identity mismatches can prevent a tool from attaching.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/diagnostic-port",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/diagnostics-in-containers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "A process dump or GC heap dump can contain sensitive application data from memory. Treat diagnostic artifacts as production data: restrict access, transfer securely, retain minimally, and dispose according to policy.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/dotnet-dump",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/dotnet-gcdump"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "Current dotnet-trace collect-linux prerequisites include .NET 10+, Linux x64/Arm64, kernel 6.4+ with CONFIG_USER_EVENTS, mounted tracefs, glibc 2.27+, and root/CAP_PERFMON or CAP_SYS_ADMIN. This is distinct from ordinary cross-platform EventPipe collect, which does not require native profiler privileges.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/dotnet-trace"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "Native AOT diagnostic support is not equivalent to CoreCLR: Microsoft documents EventPipe as optional via EventSourceSupport, partial runtime event support and CPU profiling, native debugging with symbols, and no managed heap analysis in the cited page (which specifically discusses .NET 8 limitations). Verify the deployed target version because the page may lag newer releases.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/deploying/native-aot/diagnostics"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "The official .NET System.Runtime Meter reference lists the current built-in metrics individually, all starting in .NET 9: process CPU time and working set; GC collection count, total allocated bytes, last collection committed/heap/fragmentation sizes and pause time; JIT compiled IL size, compiled method count and compilation time; ThreadPool thread/work-item/queue counts; monitor lock contentions; active timers; loaded assemblies; and thrown exceptions. Important attributes include cpu.mode, gc.heap.generation and error.type.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/built-in-metrics-runtime"
      ]
    }
  ]
